5th century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "philip-of-side", "sourceName": "Philip of Side", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "patriarchal-succession-debate-428ce", "targetName": "Patriarchal Succession Debate (428 CE)", "context": "Philip of Side was a prominent candidate for the Patriarchate of Constantinople following the death of Patriarch Sisinnius I in 428 CE."}], "places": [{"name": "Side, Turkey", "role": "Birthplace and Episcopal See"}, {"name": "Constantinople, Turkey", "role": "Episcopal candidate"}, {"name": "Pamphylia, Roman Empire", "role": "Region of birth"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Philip of Side", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "philip-of-side"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1816321", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Side"], "tags": ["Theologians & Scholars", "doctrine-development", "textual-transmission", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Philip of Side's comprehensive Christian history, though largely lost, represents a significant effort in early Byzantine historiography, providing a unique perspective and transmitting information from earlier sources to later generations. His work contributed to the ongoing shaping of Christian historical consciousness in the 5th century.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-07T19:16:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42404599336", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Philip of Side, a historian of the early Christian church, was born at Side in Pamphylia. He wrote a Christian history of which fragments survive. For some detail he relied upon the well-known Historia Ecclesiae by Eusebius of Caesarea.", "newValue": "Philip of Side, an influential but largely forgotten Christian historian of the early 5th century CE, was born in Side, a prominent city in Pamphylia (modern-day Turkey). Flourishing in the intellectual milieu of late antiquity, Philip is significant as one of the last major Greek ecclesiastical historians of the patristic era, attempting to compile a comprehensive narrative of Christian history from creation to his own time. His work bridged the foundational efforts of Eusebius of Caesarea and the later Byzantine chroniclers, reflecting the ongoing effort to define Christian identity and history within the Roman Empire.

Philip's monumental work, the *Historia Christiana* (Christian History), comprised 36 books, covering a vast span from the creation of the world up to 425 CE. While largely lost to posterity, fragments preserved primarily by the 9th-century patriarch Photius in his *Bibliotheca* reveal its ambitious scope and detailed approach. Philip extensively drew upon earlier sources, most notably the *Historia Ecclesiastica* of Eusebius, but also incorporated original material and offered distinct interpretations, sometimes differing from contemporaries like Socrates Scholasticus and Sozomen. He was also a prominent ecclesiastical figure, serving as Bishop of Side and being a candidate for the Patriarchate of Constantinople around 428 CE.

The legacy of Philip of Side, though indirect, is crucial for understanding the historiographical practices of the early Byzantine period. Despite Photius's critical assessment of his style as 'verbose and prolix,' the surviving excerpts from Philip's *Historia Christiana* offer invaluable glimpses into theological controversies, biographical details of early church figures, and historical interpretations that might otherwise be lost. His encyclopedic compilation efforts, even if imperfectly executed, demonstrate the intellectual drive to synthesize diverse historical traditions into a cohesive Christian narrative."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-07T19:16:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42404599336", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "380 CE, Side"}, {"title": "Theological debates and doctrinal development", "type": "Idea", "year": "380 CE, Side"}], "newValue": ["The institutionalization of Christianity within the Roman Empire created a strong demand for comprehensive historical narratives from a Christian perspective.", "The influential *Historia Ecclesiastica* by Eusebius of Caesarea established a foundational model for subsequent Christian historiography.", "The intellectual climate of late antiquity encouraged the compilation and synthesis of vast amounts of historical and theological knowledge."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-07T19:16:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42404599336", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Developed theological frameworks influencing doctrine", "type": "Idea", "year": "431 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "431 CE"}], "newValue": ["Philip of Side's *Historia Christiana* offered a substantial, though now fragmented, historical account of early Chri